  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nThe main part of the large upper level trough that has been \ngiving many locations much needed rainfall will finally come \nonshore and push through Nevada today through Monday morning. \nPrecipitation chances will increase to near 100% this morning and \nremain high through out the evening before finally dropping from \nwest to east during the overnight into Monday morning. Valleys and\nmost lower elevation passes will see rain, with several rounds of\nshowers and a few thunderstorms mixed in. Snow levels Saturday \nwill start this morning at around 9000 feet, however trough passes\noverhead, a cold front will bring in cooler air aloft, allowing \nsnow levels to fall around 6500 feet by Monday morning. This will \nallow for a brief changeover to snow for some of the higher passes\nlike Austin, Pinto, Sacramento, as well as for NV 225 from \nWildhorse to Owyhee. Mountain range peaks will see a good \naccumulation of snow, especially above 7500 feet. A Winter Storm \nWarning in effect Today through this evening for the Ruby \nMountains and E Humboldt range where snowfall of over 1.5 feet is \nexpected. Precipitation amounts have continued to trend steady, \nwith passes and valleys seeing rain amounts of 0.10\u201d for the Black\nRock desert, up to 0.50\u201d for the highest valleys and lower \nelevation passes. While higher elevation passes and mountain \nranges seeing up to 1.50\u201d+ of water or water equivalent. Snowfall \nas temperatures cool Sunday, with a trace to 0.3\u201d possible for the\nhigher elevation passes passes, and up to 1+ foot of snow for the\nrange peaks above 7500 feet. Monday afternoon, as the upper \ntrough departs, high pressure will build in as the trough departs \nbringing quieter weather back into the region this ridge will be \nin place at least through Tuesday. Now for Wednesday, Thursday and\nFriday of next week, models tonight have a major change up since \nyesterday, in keeping a active pattern over the great basin. As \nboth the GFS and ECMWF are in relative agreement with a pair of \nweaker upper level trough passing through Wednesday and again \nFriday bringing chances for valley rain and mountain snow showers.\nTemperature wise for the holiday weekend and into next week \nthanks to the active pattern will be relatively consistent over \nthe weekend and into next week as highs will be near to, to \nslightly below normal ranging in the mid 40s to upper 50s, with \nlows in the mid 20s to upper 30s. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for scattered showers and thunderstorms \ncontinuing on and off through Monday morning.\n\nThere is high confidence in high elevation snowfall with snow \nlevels dropping to 6500 feet by Monday morning allowing for a few \npasses to see a change over to snow with very light accumulations \npossible.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Monday \nafternoon through at least Tuesday of next week which will bring a\nquick calm for the region. \n\nThere is medium confidence for the upper level pattern for \nWednesday and Thursday of next week, as models show a upper level \ntrough pushing across Nevada bringing a low chance for showers, \nand mountain snow.\n     \nThere is low to low confidence of upper pattern evolution after \nThursday as models show varied upper air pattern with either \ntroughing or ridging taking place.",
